      "text" : "Play D3 and win in South. We have five side-suit winners and need four clubs. Cash CA with dummy's C2 and inspect the break. If both defenders follow, cash CK, playing C7, then lead a club to CQ. This produces five club tricks on a 3–2 break; on a 4–1 break, concede the remaining high club and cash the established long club. Preserve HK to reach CQ if a defensive interruption requires it, and preserve a South entry and a stopper for the club concession. If East shows void on CA, West holds all five clubs: lead C4 toward dummy; cover West's C9, CT or CJ with CQ, then lead C7 toward C8, forcing another honor while retaining CK6. If West instead plays low on the second round, play C7; if it wins, continue CQ and then lead back toward South's remaining clubs. This limits the club loss to one against West's five-card holding. If West shows void on CA, the same guarantee is unavailable: East can retain enough intermediates to restrict clubs to three tricks. Preserve CQ and the heart entries, attempt to develop clubs by ducking a round, and reassess East's actual cards and defensive returns for an additional trick; do not claim nine tricks are assured in that exceptional case. Throughout, develop clubs before spending the side-suit controls, then cash established winners.",
